00:01:07,760 --> 00:01:10,120
 Now, if we analyze this editing style as a video editor,

37
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:13,160
 it really boils down to 10 fundamental editing principles

38
00:01:13,160 --> 00:01:13,940
 that make it up.

39
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:15,140
 That might seem like a lot,

40
00:01:15,260 --> 00:01:16,300
 but once we break it down,

41
00:01:16,380 --> 00:01:17,760
 you're gonna see how easy these are.

42
00:01:17,860 --> 00:01:19,260
 The first principle is the obvious one.

43
00:01:19,420 --> 00:01:20,820
 It's the on-screen captions design.

44
00:01:20,940 --> 00:01:22,320
 The text in this style is special

45
00:01:22,320 --> 00:01:24,160
 because it emphasizes the important words.

46
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:26,300
 To do this, Arthur uses two kinds of fonts,

47
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:28,060
 a simple sans font for the main text.

48
00:01:28,220 --> 00:01:29,640
 In this case, he's using enter.

49
00:01:29,820 --> 00:01:32,520
 And a secondary script or serif font for the special text.

50
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:34,700
 In this case, he's using Playfair italic.

51
00:01:34,820 --> 00:01:36,480
 But what makes the text special isn't the font,

52
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:37,740
 it's the way he places it.

53
00:01:37,820 --> 00:01:39,040
 There's no fixed pattern here,

54
00:01:39,140 --> 00:01:40,460
 but there is simple logic.

55
00:01:40,640 --> 00:01:42,140
 He looks at the line he's about to write out,

56
00:01:42,240 --> 00:01:43,640
 which is usually five words or less.

57
00:01:43,960 --> 00:01:45,840
 He picks out the most important words in that line,

58
00:01:45,980 --> 00:01:46,660
 makes them bold,

59
00:01:46,820 --> 00:01:48,300
 either bold sans or bold serif,

60
00:01:48,380 --> 00:01:49,700
 and places them in the center.

61
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:51,580
 Then he looks for gaps around that line,

62
00:01:51,720 --> 00:01:52,660
 above it or under it,

63
00:01:52,760 --> 00:01:55,200
 and places the other words there at a smaller scale.

64
00:01:55,300 --> 00:01:57,540
 And by combining these two fonts with that placement logic,

65
00:01:57,740 --> 00:01:59,900
 we get really premium text designs like these.

66
00:02:00,040 --> 00:02:02,260
 The second principle is his B-roll and text designs.

67
00:02:02,460 --> 00:02:03,320
 To choose the right B-roll,

68
00:02:03,460 --> 00:02:05,360
 Arthur divides his script into small segments

69
00:02:05,360 --> 00:02:07,820
 and finds B-roll that shows what he's talking about.

70
00:02:07,920 --> 00:02:09,440
 If he mentions fighting and resisting,

71
00:02:09,660 --> 00:02:11,340
 he's gonna find B-roll of a guy fighting

72
00:02:11,340 --> 00:02:12,520
 and use it as the background.

73
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:14,220
 But he doesn't just drop the B-roll in,

74
00:02:14,420 --> 00:02:16,260
 he applies a light paper texture to it,

75
00:02:16,420 --> 00:02:17,160
 darkens the edges,

76
00:02:17,160 --> 00:02:18,680
 and even blurs the edges a little,

77
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:21,580
 so the viewer focuses on the main focal point of the B-roll.

78
00:02:21,680 --> 00:02:23,000
 Then finally, and most importantly,

79
00:02:23,220 --> 00:02:24,840
 the text is placed on top of the B-roll

80
00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:26,600
 using the same cinematic text logic

81
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:27,420
 from the first principle,

82
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:28,700
 so it describes the scene.

83
00:02:28,780 --> 00:02:29,920
 The third principle is similar,

84
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:30,900
 but slightly different.

85
00:02:31,040 --> 00:02:32,480
 These are his painting animations,

86
00:02:32,760 --> 00:02:33,800
 and these are my favorite.

87
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:35,420
 Arthur uses paintings with text

88
00:02:35,420 --> 00:02:37,040
 to deliver his philosophical points,

89
00:02:37,180 --> 00:02:38,340
 and it's just pure art.

90
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:39,780
 He finds a painting that describes

91
00:02:39,780 --> 00:02:40,580
 what he's talking about,

92
00:02:40,720 --> 00:02:42,720
 then he applies a paint and rough paper texture

93
00:02:42,720 --> 00:02:44,500
 to the painting to make it feel more real.

94
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:45,900
 He adds cinematic text

95
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:47,320
 around the painting's main subject

96
00:02:47,320 --> 00:02:48,660
 to describe the scene even more,

97
00:02:48,960 --> 00:02:50,100
 and tops it all off

98
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:52,000
 with a quick camera slide-in animation

99
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:52,900
 to reveal it.

100
00:02:52,980 --> 00:02:54,340
 This is just the most brilliant way

101
00:02:54,340 --> 00:02:55,060
 to describe a scene.

102
00:02:55,140 --> 00:02:55,760
 The fourth principle

103
00:02:55,760 --> 00:02:57,520
 is the first kind of animation that we see.

104
00:02:57,700 --> 00:02:59,400
 It's his line and text animations.

105
00:02:59,580 --> 00:03:00,180
 In these scenes,

106
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:01,820
 there's a line that leads up to the text,

107
00:03:02,040 --> 00:03:03,160
 gets interrupted by it,

108
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:04,420
 and then continues the thought

109
00:03:04,420 --> 00:03:05,440
 into other text

110
00:03:05,440 --> 00:03:06,660
 that's related to the first.

111
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:07,420
 To create them,

112
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:09,020
 we start off with either a black

113
00:03:09,020 --> 00:03:09,940
 or white background

114
00:03:09,940 --> 00:03:11,320
 with a paper texture on it.

115
00:03:11,380 --> 00:03:12,720
 Then he draws out a simple line

116
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:13,860
 in an accent blue color,

117
00:03:14,060 --> 00:03:15,280
 and he places cinematic text

118
00:03:15,280 --> 00:03:16,060
 to interrupt it.

119
00:03:16,180 --> 00:03:16,920
 The cameras track

120
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:17,840
 where the line goes,

121
00:03:17,960 --> 00:03:18,780
 so the viewer follows

122
00:03:18,780 --> 00:03:20,160
 exactly what Arthur is saying.

123
00:03:20,240 --> 00:03:20,820
 The fifth principle

124
00:03:20,820 --> 00:03:21,640
 is very similar,

125
00:03:21,820 --> 00:03:22,680
 but it's his line

126
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:24,040
 and illustrations animations.

127
00:03:24,360 --> 00:03:25,660
 These start with the same background

128
00:03:25,660 --> 00:03:26,380
 and the same line,

129
00:03:26,560 --> 00:03:27,580
 but now the line leads

130
00:03:27,580 --> 00:03:28,380
 into illustrations.

131
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:30,140
 Sometimes the line draws horizontally

132
00:03:30,140 --> 00:03:31,020
 with a white dot

133
00:03:31,020 --> 00:03:32,380
 to mark where the object is,

134
00:03:32,740 --> 00:03:33,940
 cinematic text to describe it,

135
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:35,240
 and an illustration like this one

136
00:03:35,240 --> 00:03:36,260
 at the bottom to show it.

137
00:03:36,320 --> 00:03:36,820
 Other times,

138
00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:38,060
 he starts the line with text,

139
00:03:38,180 --> 00:03:39,040
 then draws the line

140
00:03:39,040 --> 00:03:39,760
 in a zigzag

141
00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:40,900
 and pans the camera

142
00:03:40,900 --> 00:03:42,040
 to reveal objects

143
00:03:42,040 --> 00:03:43,020
 on both sides of it.

144
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:43,800
 He uses this one

145
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:44,780
 when he's showing progression

146
00:03:44,780 --> 00:03:45,800
 through multiple phases,

147
00:03:46,020 --> 00:03:46,900
 and sometimes he'll even

148
00:03:46,900 --> 00:03:48,100
 have the line lead straight

149
00:03:48,100 --> 00:03:49,560
 up to the subject of the scene.

150
00:03:49,620 --> 00:03:50,100
 And by the way,

151
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:51,320
 understanding these principles

152
00:03:51,320 --> 00:03:52,820
 of how this style is made

153
00:03:52,820 --> 00:03:53,860
 is super important

154
00:03:53,860 --> 00:03:55,340
 if you want to get stunning results

155
00:03:55,340 --> 00:03:56,660
 like this later in the video.

156
00:03:56,760 --> 00:03:57,180
 So let's go ahead

157
00:03:57,180 --> 00:03:58,020
 and wrap up the last five

158
00:03:58,020 --> 00:03:58,940
 and show you how we do that.

159
00:03:59,020 --> 00:03:59,720
 The sixth principle

160
00:03:59,720 --> 00:04:00,920
 is text-centric scenes.

161
00:04:01,100 --> 00:04:01,760
 They start with either

162
00:04:01,760 --> 00:04:03,020
 a white or a blue background

163
00:04:03,020 --> 00:04:03,860
 with a very rough

164
00:04:03,860 --> 00:04:04,900
 paper texture on it.

165
00:04:04,980 --> 00:04:06,320
 Then there's two quotation marks

166
00:04:06,320 --> 00:04:07,380
 around the center of the screen

167
00:04:07,380 --> 00:04:08,680
 and cinematic text in the middle

168
00:04:08,680 --> 00:04:09,520
 that writes out

169
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:10,480
 what the scene is saying.

170
00:04:10,580 --> 00:04:11,100
 It's very simple

171
00:04:11,100 --> 00:04:11,940
 and straight to the bottom.

172
00:04:12,040 --> 00:04:12,740
 The seventh principle

173
00:04:12,740 --> 00:04:14,240
 is where things get interesting.

174
00:04:14,420 --> 00:04:16,160
 These are the center illustration scenes

175
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:16,820
 where an illustration

176
00:04:16,820 --> 00:04:18,200
 takes up the center of the screen

177
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:19,320
 with text around it.

178
00:04:19,400 --> 00:04:20,440
 Arthur takes the main idea

179
00:04:20,440 --> 00:04:20,860
 of a script,

180
00:04:21,060 --> 00:04:21,800
 creates an illustration

181
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:22,480
 that shows it,

182
00:04:22,560 --> 00:04:23,480
 let's say it's a person,

183
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:24,520
 and places it on his

184
00:04:24,520 --> 00:04:25,940
 white paper textured background

185
00:04:25,940 --> 00:04:26,620
 in a rough,

186
00:04:26,860 --> 00:04:27,960
 painted, noisy texture.

187
00:04:28,120 --> 00:04:29,160
 It takes up the lower half

188
00:04:29,160 --> 00:04:29,620
 of the screen

189
00:04:29,620 --> 00:04:31,280
 and bleeds into the upper middle.

190
00:04:31,380 --> 00:04:32,740
 And it always looks so abstract

191
00:04:32,740 --> 00:04:33,660
 with these edges

192
00:04:33,660 --> 00:04:35,320
 that lead into the rest of the scene.

193
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:36,820
 Then he uses the rest of the scene

194
00:04:36,820 --> 00:04:37,800
 for cinematic text

195
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:38,900
 related to the subject.

196
00:04:39,120 --> 00:04:40,500
 Sometimes it's a top-down image

197
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:41,120
 of a brain

198
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:42,240
 or a circular pattern

199
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:43,140
 made of thin lines

200
00:04:43,140 --> 00:04:44,500
 with clean text around it.

201
00:04:44,580 --> 00:04:45,160
 And my favorites

202
00:04:45,160 --> 00:04:47,000
 are the side profiles of characters

203
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:48,040
 where Arthur uses

204
00:04:48,040 --> 00:04:49,460
 a rough cloth texture

205
00:04:49,460 --> 00:04:50,660
 to contrast the character

206
00:04:50,660 --> 00:04:51,620
 against the background

207
00:04:51,620 --> 00:04:52,600
 so it grabs attention.

208
00:04:52,740 --> 00:04:53,380
 There's a lot of scenes

209
00:04:53,380 --> 00:04:54,000
 in this category,

210
00:04:54,160 --> 00:04:55,420
 but they all fit the Arthur look

211
00:04:55,420 --> 00:04:56,960
 by following the same textures,

212
00:04:57,240 --> 00:04:57,860
 the same edges,

213
00:04:58,020 --> 00:04:58,740
 and the same colors.

214
00:04:58,960 --> 00:04:59,660
 The eighth principle

215
00:04:59,660 --> 00:05:00,900
 is the side illustration

216
00:05:00,900 --> 00:05:01,720
 and text scenes.

217
00:05:02,020 --> 00:05:03,300
 These use the sides of the screen

218
00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:04,300
 to deliver the message.

219
00:05:04,460 --> 00:05:05,640
 It's the same textured background

220
00:05:05,640 --> 00:05:07,040
 and the same abstract characters,

221
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:08,840
 but now they sit on one side

222
00:05:08,840 --> 00:05:09,400
 of the screen

223
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:10,400
 with cinematic text

224
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:11,240
 on the other side

225
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:12,720
 so the scene looks complete.

226
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:14,200
 Sometimes it's a half circle

227
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:15,360
 with a rough cloth texture

228
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:16,420
 on the left side of the screen

229
00:05:16,420 --> 00:05:17,220
 with clean text

230
00:05:17,220 --> 00:05:18,220
 rotating around it.

231
00:05:18,280 --> 00:05:19,260
 Arthur uses these scenes

232
00:05:19,260 --> 00:05:20,220
 whenever he wants to show

233
00:05:20,220 --> 00:05:20,860
 two elements

234
00:05:20,860 --> 00:05:21,660
 at the same time.

235
00:05:21,740 --> 00:05:22,000
 Finally,

236
00:05:22,100 --> 00:05:22,860
 the last two principles

237
00:05:22,860 --> 00:05:24,060
 are rare but useful.

238
00:05:24,180 --> 00:05:24,780
 The ninth principle

239
00:05:24,780 --> 00:05:26,260
 is the object and text scene

240
00:05:26,260 --> 00:05:27,160
 where Arthur shows

241
00:05:27,160 --> 00:05:27,880
 a single element

242
00:05:27,880 --> 00:05:28,960
 with text around it

243
00:05:28,960 --> 00:05:29,940
 as a part of the story,

244
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:30,640
 paper background,

245
00:05:30,960 --> 00:05:32,100
 cinematic text around the element,

246
00:05:32,280 --> 00:05:33,340
 and rough reveal animations.

247
00:05:33,340 --> 00:05:34,420
 And the tenth principle

248
00:05:34,420 --> 00:05:36,140
 is the graph and statistics scenes

249
00:05:36,140 --> 00:05:37,340
 where Arthur uses graphs

250
00:05:37,340 --> 00:05:37,960
 to show the viewer

251
00:05:37,960 --> 00:05:38,960
 what the numbers look like.

252
00:05:39,040 --> 00:05:40,280
 He uses a flat dark background

253
00:05:40,280 --> 00:05:40,880
 with a texture

254
00:05:40,880 --> 00:05:42,520
 and a grid with thin white lines

255
00:05:42,520 --> 00:05:43,960
 that glow to set up the graph.

256
00:05:44,060 --> 00:05:44,300
 Typically,

257
00:05:44,400 --> 00:05:45,520
 he also uses a blue ball

258
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:46,420
 to represent a line

259
00:05:46,420 --> 00:05:47,060
 with the camera

260
00:05:47,060 --> 00:05:48,220
 following it as it moves.

261
00:05:48,380 --> 00:05:48,560
 Now,

262
00:05:48,720 --> 00:05:49,680
 these ten principles

263
00:05:49,680 --> 00:05:50,560
 all come together

264
00:05:50,560 --> 00:05:51,360
 in one video

